</style>
  </div></section>
<section style="padding:0 24px;">
  <div style="max-width:860px;margin:0 auto 56px;">
    <h2 class="section-heading" style="font-size:clamp(22px,3vw,32px);margin:0 0 18px;">Captive generation is the primary supply for Kano industry</h2>
    <div class="rich"><p>Kano sits in the KEDCO distribution franchise, and industrial users across the estates plan their shifts around available generation rather than around the feeder. Most plants are sized to run full production on their own sets, with a second unit for redundancy, which means the equipment is on prime duty and not standby duty. A set running 16 hours a day, six days a week, accumulates close to 5,000 running hours in a year, so it passes through service intervals three to five times faster than a standby unit on the same nameplate.</p><p>Prime duty in this environment produces a predictable set of failures. Injector fouling and turbocharger deterioration on poor quality diesel, liner glazing and wet stacking where a large set spends long periods below 40 percent of rating, and alternator insulation degradation where a plant runs unbalanced single phase loads across three phases. Load bank testing and a corrected load profile fix most of that, and it is cheaper than the top end rebuild that follows if it is ignored.</p><p>The cost that matters is not the repair. It is the tannery drum line, the mill or the extruder standing idle while a crankshaft is measured. That is why we scope the work in advance and agree the outage window before mobilising, rather than opening an engine and then discovering what it needs.</p></div>
  </div>
</section>
<section style="padding:0 24px;">
  <div style="max-width:860px;margin:0 auto 56px;">
    <h2 class="section-heading" style="font-size:clamp(22px,3vw,32px);margin:0 0 18px;">Harmattan dust, ambient heat and site derating in Kano</h2>
    <div class="rich"><p>Kano sits at roughly 470 metres above sea level, and ambient temperatures through the March to May period regularly sit above 40 degrees Celsius. Both facts move the site rating of your plant away from the nameplate figure. Under the ISO 3046 and ISO 8528 correction rules, altitude and intake air temperature together can remove a meaningful slice of available output, which is how a plant that believes it holds 20 percent spare capacity finds itself running flat out at peak load.</p><p>The harmattan compounds it. From November to February the north easterly carries fine dust that loads air filters, blinds radiator and intercooler cores, packs alternator windings and shows up as silica in oil analysis. Air filter differential pressure has to be monitored rather than assumed, pre-cleaners and cyclonic stages need to be specified properly, and turbine inlet filtration on gas machines needs a staged arrangement with a realistic replacement plan. Radiator core cleaning intervals in Kano should be indexed to the season, not fixed once a year.</p><p>Combine a derated site rating with a fouled cooling pack and the result is high exhaust gas temperature, reduced load acceptance and shortened component life. A <a href="/power-plant-audit-nigeria/">power plant audit</a> measures the real headroom, the real specific fuel consumption and the real thermal margins, so that any decision on a new set or an overhaul is made on data from your own site.</p></div>
  </div>
</section>
<section style="padding:0 24px;">
  <div style="max-width:860px;margin:0 auto 56px;">
    <h2 class="section-heading" style="font-size:clamp(22px,3vw,32px);margin:0 0 18px;">Spares, mobilisation and outage planning for a Kano site</h2>
    <div class="rich"><p>Kano is roughly 1,000 kilometres by road from Lagos, and there is no specialist turbine and rotating equipment provider in the city to fall back on. That distance dictates the method: nothing gets diagnosed on arrival. We carry out a site survey first, take clearances, bearing readings, oil samples and photographs of the nameplates and running gear, then issue a technical proposal with a defined parts list, labour content and a fixed window before any engineer travels.</p><p>The job is kitted in Lagos and moved as one consignment, with critical wear items, gaskets, bearings, seals and consumables included rather than ordered mid outage. Where lead times on OEM parts are long, we say so in the proposal and offer exchange or refurbished options with the trade offs written out, so procurement can compare like with like. Engineers fly into Mallam Aminu Kano International Airport while heavy freight and tooling move by road ahead of them.</p><p>We plan Kano visits alongside our <a href="/turbine-services-kaduna/">Kaduna</a> coverage where schedules allow, which shortens mobilisation for planned maintenance contracts on the northern corridor. Indicative ranges for overhauls and maintenance contracts are set out on our <a href="/generator-turbine-maintenance-cost/">maintenance cost page</a>. To start, book a plant assessment and we will return a written technical proposal.</p></div>
  </div>
</section>
<section style="padding:72px 24px;background:#EAF5FB;">
  <div style="max-width:860px;margin:0 auto;">
    <span class="kicker">FAQ</span>
    <h2 class="section-heading" style="font-size:clamp(24px,3.5vw,36px);margin:0 0 32px;">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    <div style="display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:16px;">
      <div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid #e3ded4;border-radius:8px;padding:24px 28px;">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Nunito Sans',sans-serif;font-size:18px;font-weight:800;color:#0A3D5C;margin:0 0 10px;">Are you based in Kano, or do you travel in from Lagos?</h3>
        <p style="font-size:15px;line-height:1.75;color:#555;margin:0;">Our engineering base is Lagos and we serve Kano State as a covered region, with work planned rather than improvised. Planned maintenance visits are scheduled in advance and often batched along the Kaduna and Kano corridor to keep mobilisation efficient. For breakdown response, engineers can fly into Mallam Aminu Kano International Airport at short notice while tooling and heavy spares move by road, which is why we hold agreed critical spares lists for contract sites.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid #e3ded4;border-radius:8px;padding:24px 28px;">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Nunito Sans',sans-serif;font-size:18px;font-weight:800;color:#0A3D5C;margin:0 0 10px;">How should harmattan change our service intervals in Kano?</h3>
        <p style="font-size:15px;line-height:1.75;color:#555;margin:0;">Treat the November to February period as a separate maintenance season rather than applying one annual schedule. Air filter changes should be driven by measured differential pressure, radiator and intercooler cores should be inspected and cleaned more frequently, and oil sampling should be read for silica and soot rather than only for hours in service. On gas turbines, inlet filtration staging and compressor washing frequency both need reviewing before the dust season begins.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid #e3ded4;border-radius:8px;padding:24px 28px;">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Nunito Sans',sans-serif;font-size:18px;font-weight:800;color:#0A3D5C;margin:0 0 10px;">What does a generator overhaul cost for a Kano plant?</h3>
        <p style="font-size:15px;line-height:1.75;color:#555;margin:0;">A top end overhaul on a 500 kVA class set typically falls between NGN 4,500,000 and NGN 9,000,000 (about USD 2,800 to USD 5,600) depending on parts condition and whether the head assemblies are rebuilt or exchanged. Mobilisation of a crew and tooling from Lagos to Kano generally adds NGN 350,000 to NGN 900,000 (about USD 220 to USD 560), which reduces materially when the visit is scheduled with other northern work. Turbine overhauls are quoted only after inspection, because scope depends on hot section and rotor condition. Figures are indicative and confirmed in a written proposal after a site survey.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid #e3ded4;border-radius:8px;padding:24px 28px;">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Nunito Sans',sans-serif;font-size:18px;font-weight:800;color:#0A3D5C;margin:0 0 10px;">Should we wait for pipeline gas before investing in new generation in Kano?</h3>
        <p style="font-size:15px;line-height:1.75;color:#555;margin:0;">The Ajaokuta to Kaduna to Kano pipeline is under construction and CNG is already being trucked to northern industrial users, so fuel flexibility is a live commercial question in Kano rather than a theoretical one. The practical answer depends on your load factor, current diesel spend and how long your existing sets can safely run. We assess whether your installed sets are candidates for dual fuel conversion, whether a gas engine or gas turbine makes sense at your load profile, and what the whole life cost looks like against continuing on diesel, then put the comparison in writing.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
<section style="background:linear-gradient(135deg, #0E4D6E 0%, #16648C 50%, #0E4D6E 100%);padding:64px 24px;text-align:center;">
  <h2 style="font-family:'Nunito Sans',sans-serif;font-size:clamp(26px,4vw,40px);font-weight:800;color:#fff;margin-bottom:16px;">Trouble in Kano? We Can Be On Our Way.</h2>
  <p style="font-size:16px;color:rgba(255,255,255,0.8);margin-bottom:28px;max-width:520px;margin-left:auto;margin-right:auto;">Written technical proposals. Outage support available.</p>
